    "uid": "6964cd6a647196b85be5180a34a4c7c8",
    "title": "Audit or syslog configuration tampered",
    "desc": "A program edited the audit or syslog configuration on disk - /etc/audit (auditd.conf, rules.d, audit.rules), /etc/audisp, /etc/rsyslog(.conf / .d), /etc/syslog-ng - via a file tool, or any process whose file access touched those paths. Quietly altering the rules that decide what gets logged, rather than stopping the daemon outright, is a subtler indicator-blocking step (MITRE T1562.006): the service keeps running so a `systemctl status` looks healthy, but the events an attacker cares about are no longer recorded. disable_logging catches stopping the service and tamper_banken catches auditctl flushing live rules; this catches the on-disk config edit.",
